    "rationale": "Key improvement over round 1: use both accumulator tiles (rows 0-15 and 16-31) to keep two column blocks live simultaneously, pairing blocks (0,1) and (2,3) in a loop over p (block a=2p, b=2p+1) plus a final flat block 4. Within a pair, compute order is c0a (A0*B0a overwrite acc rows 0-15), c0b (A0*B0b overwrite acc rows 16-31), c1a (A1*B1a accumulate rows 0-15), c1b (A1*B1b accumulate rows 16-31). A0 stays resident at spad row 0 across both chunk-0 computes (one A0 load serves two blocks) and A1 likewise serves both chunk-1 computes, so each pair needs only 6 mvins instead of 8. Per-block K order is preserved: chunk 0 (A cols 0-15, B rows 0-15) overwrites, chunk 1 (A offset 16, B offset 1280+16*block) accumulates, satisfying the ordered-reduction contract; disjoint accumulator tiles keep the two blocks independent. Spad uses exactly rows 0-15 (A slot) and 16-31 (B slot); all tile starts are multiples of 16 within the 32-row capacities. Every compute is immediately preceded by its own preload with accumulated=false and bd_spad_addr=4294967295, matching the accepted round-1 pattern; accumulate-mode computes and all mvouts follow initialized accumulator contents. out_addr values set bit 31 (accumulator) + bit 29 (full width), plus bit 30 for accumulate, with low bits 0 or 16 selecting the accumulator tile; mvout acc_addr uses the same layout. C offsets are 16*block with store stride 320 bytes (80 int32 per row). Totals: 16 mvins (6 A + 10 B, each B tile loaded exactly once) = 4096 DMA input bytes versus 5120 in round 1, and 46 expanded commands (2x16 loop body + 9 final block + 4 configs + 1 fence)."
  },
